  "notes": [
   "Parts from step 1 are unchanged; no part was added or dropped.",
   "The tutorial covers many exercises, but both images show only the light-sensing circuit, so only that one is built.",
   "Pin mapping from the Fritzing image: the Pi is drawn rotated 180\u00b0. Red goes to pin 1 (3V3.1) and green to pin 7 (GPIO4), both in the upper row. Black goes to pin 6 (GND.1) in the lower row. This matches the code LightSensor(4).",
   "Breadboard: red goes to column 1, green to column 3 and black to column 5, all in row a. The LDR spans columns 1 and 3, as in the second image. The capacitor spans columns 3 and 5, with its positive (long) leg in column 3 on the same strip as an LDR leg, as the manual says. The circuit is 3V3 \u2192 LDR \u2192 GPIO4 node \u2192 capacitor \u2192 GND.",
   "Exact rows for the component legs (row e for the LDR, row d for the capacitor) are chosen only to avoid two legs sharing a hole. Any hole in the same column strip is electrically the same.",
   "For the male-to-female jumpers, port a is assumed to be the female end on the Pi header and port b the male end in the breadboard.",
   "The capacitor is catalogue type capacitor-10uf with value=1uF, because no 1\u03bcF type exists."
  ]
 },
